hadiths[99]{hadithnumber,text}:
"7198","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that a man sat with the Prophet ﷺ, who asked about his origin and he declared himself a Berber, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ gestured with his elbow ordering him to rise, then turned to the companions with a judgment about their faith. > **""From where are you? Rise from me. Verily faith does not pass beyond their throats.""**"
"7199","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a ruling for one who sets aside charity but finds no recipient except a Berber. > **""Whoever brings out charity but finds none except a Berber, let him return it.""**"
"7200","A freedman of Rafi ibn Thabit reports that a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ bought a Berber slave girl for two hundred dinars and sent her to Abu Muhammad al-Badri رضي الله عنه, a veteran of Badr, who received her and declared her among the Magians forbidden by the Prophet ﷺ along with the idolaters."
"7201","Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divide wickedness into seventy portions, assigning the overwhelming share to the Berbers and a single share to jinn and mankind. > **""Wickedness is seventy parts: one part is among the jinn and mankind, and sixty-nine are among the Berbers.""**"
"7202","A variant narration reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing Allah's division of wickedness into seventy parts, placing sixty-nine in the Berbers and one among the rest of people. > **""Allah divided wickedness into seventy parts, placing sixty-nine parts in the Berbers and one part for mankind.""**"
"7203","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a division of wickedness assigning sixty-nine parts to the Berbers and one part to jinn and mankind. > **""Wickedness is seventy parts: for the Berbers sixty-nine parts, and for the jinn and mankind one part.""**"
"7204","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding purchase of slaves with partnership in their provision, while warning against the Zanj due to their short lives and scant provision. > **""Buy slaves and share with them in their provision, and beware of the Zanj, for their lives are short and their provision is little.""**"
"7205","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Blacks were mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, who responded by leaving their affair aside and reducing the Black man to his belly and private part. > **""Leave me aside from the Blacks, for the Black man is only for his belly and his private part.""**"
"7206","Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confine the concern of the Black man to desire and food. > **""The Black man is only for his private part and his belly.""**"
"7207","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports being asked what prevents the Abyssinians of Banu al-Mughirah from coming except fear of rejection, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ declared no good in the Abyssinians when hungry or full, while affirming two fine traits in them. > **""There is no good in the Abyssinians. When they hunger they steal, and when they are full they commit adultery, yet in them are two fine traits: feeding food and valor in battle.""**"
"7208","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the Black man stealing when hungry and committing adultery when full, while affirming two virtues in them. > **""Verily the Black man, when he hungers he steals, and when he is full he commits adultery, yet in them are two traits: true generosity and courage.""**"
"7209","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding adoption of Black slaves, informing that three among them are leaders of Paradise. > **""Take the Blacks, for three among them are leaders of the people of Paradise: Luqman the Wise, the Negus, and Bilal the caller to prayer.""**"
"7210","Umayr reports that Sahl ibn Sakhr رضي الله عنه, a Companion, advised him as a son that when he owns the price of a slave he should buy a slave, for fortunes lie in the forelocks of men."
"7211","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding one who buys a slave girl to first feed her sweets as most pleasant for her soul. > **""When one of you buys a slave girl, let the first thing he feeds her be sweets, for it is most pleasant for her soul.""**"
"7212","Yazid ibn Jariyah رضي الله عنه reports that in the Farewell Pilgrimage the Prophet ﷺ repeated his charge concerning owned slaves, commanding feeding and clothing like oneself, and selling rather than torturing an unforgiven offender. > **""Your slaves, your slaves, your slaves. Feed them from what you eat and clothe them from what you wear. If they commit a sin you do not wish to forgive, then sell the servants of Allah and do not torture them.""**"
"7213","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning that an evil owner of slaves shall not enter Paradise, then when reminded that this nation holds most slaves and orphans, commanding honor like children and sustenance, and naming the horse for jihad and the suffices slave as worldly benefit, with prayer establishing brotherhood. > **""One evil in ownership shall not enter Paradise. Yes, so honor them as you honor your children and feed them from what you eat. A horse you tether to fight upon in the path of Allah, and a slave who suffices you; when he prays, he is your brother, when he prays, he is your brother.""**"
"7214","A man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring slaves to be brothers owed rectitude, mutual aid in mastered work, and assistance in overwhelming work. > **""Your brothers, so do good to them, seek their help in what they can bear, and aid them in what overcomes them.""**"
"7215","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruling for slaves that goodness be accepted, evil pardoned, and the overpowering sold. > **""Concerning slaves: if they do good, accept it, and if they do evil, pardon it, and if they overcome you, sell them.""**"
"7216","Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ three dues of the slave upon his master concerning prayer, food, and satiation. > **""For the slave upon his master are three traits: he does not hasten him from his prayer, nor remove him from his food, and he satiates him with full satiation.""**"
"7217","Kab ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports his last meeting with the Prophet ﷺ five nights before his death, hearing of the intimate friend Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah and Allah taking his companion as intimate, prohibition of graves as mosques, repeated conveyance and witnessing, a faint, then a charge to fear Allah concerning owned slaves with full bellies, covered backs, and gentle speech. > **""There was no prophet except he had an intimate friend from his nation, and my intimate friend is Abu Bakr ibn Abi Quhafah, while Allah has taken your companion as intimate. Verily the nations before you took the graves of their prophets as mosques, and I forbid you from that. O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, bear witness.""** > **""Allah, Allah concerning what your right hands possess. Fill their bellies, clothe their backs, and soften speech to them.""**"
"7218","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the general bequest of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was prayer and owned slaves, repeated until his chest rattled in death and his tongue failed to utter it. > **""Prayer, and what your right hands possess.""**"
"7219","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports a man who bought a slave asking the Prophet ﷺ what to do with him, receiving the ruling of Islamic brotherhood in food and clothing, with sale upon dislike. > **""He is your brother in Islam. Feed him from what you eat and clothe him from what you wear, and if you dislike him, sell him.""**"
"7220","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports that Talhah spoke harshly to Amir ibn Fuhayrah, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ restrained him by shared Badr service and the superiority of the best to their freedmen. > **""Slowly, O Talhah, for he witnessed Badr as you witnessed it, and the best of you are the best of you to their freedmen.""**"
"7221","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ returning from Khaybar with two boys, permitting Ali رضي الله عنه to take whichever he wished then selecting one with prohibition of striking for witnessed prayer, and giving Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه a boy with a charge of goodness, whom Abu Dharr freed and defended by that charge when questioned. > **""Take whichever of them you wish. Take this one and do not strike him, for I saw him praying on our return from Khaybar, and I have been forbidden from striking the people of prayer.""** > **""Treat him with goodness. What became of the boy?""**"
"7222","A variant reports Ali رضي الله عنه asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a servant and being offered selection among three in the house. > **""In the house are three; choose one.""**"
"7223","A further variant reports the Prophet ﷺ giving Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه a youth with command to feed and clothe alike, whereupon Abu Dharr split his garment between himself and the boy, and when questioned declared his emancipation, earning the Prophet's ﷺ supplication for reward. > **""Feed him from what you eat and clothe him from what you wear. Why do I see your garment thus? Yes. May Allah reward you, O Abu Dharr.""**"
"7224","Anas رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ giving Ali and Fatimah رضي الله عنهما a boy with command to treat him well for witnessed prayer. > **""Treat him well, for I saw him praying.""**"
"7225","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ commanding feeding and clothing of slaves from one's own food and dress. > **""Feed them from what you eat and clothe them from what you wear.""**"
"7226","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ commanding one whose servant brings food to draw him near, seat him, and feed him a morsel, for he endured its heat and smoke. > **""When the servant of one of you brings food, let him draw him near and seat him upon it and feed him a morsel, for he endured its heat and its smoke.""**"
"7227","Abu al-Zubayr reports asking Jabir رضي الله عنه about a man's servant who sufficed him hardship and heat, receiving the Prophet's ﷺ command to invite him, or at least place food in his hand if eating together is disliked. > **""Invite him, and if one of you dislikes eating with him, let him feed him in his hand.""**"
"7228","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ deeming it unbefitting for a man whose slave endured the heat and cold of his food to then isolate him when it is served. > **""It is not fitting for a man whose slave endures the heat of his food and its cold, then when it is present he isolates him from it.""**"
"7229","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruling that when one's slave prepares food enduring its heat and labor then presents it, he be invited to eat together, or else given some by hand. > **""When the slave of one of you prepares food, enduring its heat and its labor, then brings it near to him, let him invite him to eat with him, and if he refuses, let him place in his hand from what he prepared.""**"
"7230","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring no charity superior to charity given to a slave under an evil owner. > **""There is no charity more virtuous than charity given to a slave under an evil owner.""**"
"7231","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports a man asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether to strike his offending, oppressing servant, receiving the command of daily seventyfold pardon. > **""Pardon him seventy times each day.""**"
"7232","Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning that one who strikes his slave unjustly shall be retaliated against on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever strikes his slave unjustly shall be retaliated against on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"7233","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding striking of slaves, for one knows not what it corresponds to. > **""Do not strike slaves, for you know not what you coincide with.""**"
"7234","Kab ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports striking his shepherd slave girl in the face after a wolf ate a sheep, then regretting and asking the Prophet ﷺ about freeing her if believing, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ questioned her about his identity and Allah, received her affirmation of his messengership and Allah above the heaven, and judged her believing. > **""Who am I? Who is Allah? Free her, for she is a believer.""**"
"7235","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that one who mutilates his slave or burns him with fire renders him free as a freedman of Allah and His Messenger. The narrator adds that Sindar, reportedly castrated, was freed by him, then honored by Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه after the Prophet's ﷺ death and by Umar رضي الله عنه, who wrote to Amr ibn al-As in Egypt to honor him and guard the Prophet's ﷺ charge. > **""Whoever mutilates his slave or burns him with fire, he is free, and he is the freedman of Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"7236","Sindar رضي الله عنه reports being with al-Zinba ibn Salamah, who in anger castrated him and cut his nose and ears, whereupon he informed the Prophet ﷺ, who rebuked Zinba severely and freed him from him, then upon request charged every Muslim with his care. > **""I charge every Muslim concerning you.""**"
"7237","Amr ibn Hurayth رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that whatever one lightens from his worker's labor counts as reward in his scales. > **""Whatever you lighten from your worker's labor, its reward is in your scales.""**"
"7238","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that a slave obeying Allah and his masters enters Paradise seventy autumns before his masters, whereupon the master pleads his worldly ownership and the Lord answers with recompense according to deeds. > **""A slave who obeys Allah and obeys his masters, Allah admits him to Paradise seventy autumns before his masters. The master says: My Lord, this was my slave in the world. He says: I recompensed him for his deed and recompensed you for your deed.""**"
"7239","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing a servant entering Paradise and seeing his slave above his degree, questioning his Lord, and receiving the answer of recompense by deeds. > **""A servant entered Paradise and saw his slave above his degree, so he said: My Lord, this is my slave above my degree? He said: Yes, I recompensed him for his deed and recompensed you for your deed.""**"
"7240","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ naming the obedient slave to Allah and masters as the first hastener to Paradise. > **""The first hastener to Paradise is a slave who obeys Allah and obeys his masters.""**"
"7241","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning that a slave dying in desertion enters the Fire even if killed in the path of Allah. > **""A slave who dies in his desertion enters the Fire, even if killed in the path of Allah.""**"
"7242","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reports a Bedouin asking the Prophet ﷺ for a deed admitting to Paradise, receiving the brief yet weighty counsel to free the soul and ransom the neck, explained as sole freeing versus assisting in freeing, along with the dripping grant, kindness to the oppressive kin, feeding the hungry, watering the thirsty, commanding good, forbidding evil, and otherwise restraining the tongue except for good. > **""If you have shortened speech you have posed a weighty question: free the soul and ransom the neck. No, freeing the soul is to single-handedly free it, and ransoming the neck is to assist in freeing it, along with the dripping grant and kindness to the oppressive kin. If you cannot do that, feed the hungry, give drink to the thirsty, command good and forbid evil, and if you cannot do that, restrain your tongue except for good.""**"
"7243","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention charity as freeing the neck and ransoming it, explaining the former as freeing it oneself and the latter as assisting therein, then naming the dripping grant and kindness to kin for one unable to do so. > **""Among charity is freeing the neck and ransoming it. No, freeing it is to free it, and ransoming it is to assist therein. Then a dripping grant or kindness to kin.""**"
"7244","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising Divine shade on a shadeless Day to one who aids a warrior in Allah's path, a debtor in hardship, or a contracted slave in his ransom. > **""Whoever aids a warrior in the path of Allah Almighty, or a debtor in his hardship, or a contracted slave in his ransom, Allah shades him on a Day when there is no shade except His.""**"
"7245","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports a man who embraced Islam but was chained by his family fearing his following the Prophet ﷺ after the migration, who wrote informing of his Islam seeking deliverance, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ sent six men on a camel hoping to find a helper's house, then freed him. > **""Perhaps you will find in someone's house one who aids you concerning him.""**"
"7246","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extolling the loaned grant arriving morning and evening with reward, likening the loaned she-camel to freeing a red slave and the loaned sheep to freeing a black slave. > **""The best charity is the loaned grant arriving morning with reward and returning evening with reward: the loan of a she-camel is like freeing a red slave, and the loan of a sheep is like freeing a black slave.""**"
"7247","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports a man asking the Prophet ﷺ about the best deed and the most rewarding neck to free, receiving faith and jihad, then the priciest and most precious to its people, then assisting a craftsman or the unskilled, then withholding oneself from evil as charity for oneself. > **""Faith in Allah and jihad in His path. The dearest in price and most precious to its people. Assist a craftsman or work for the unskilled. Restrain yourself from evil, for it is a good charity you give for yourself.""**"
"7248","Sad, freedman of Abu Bakr رضي الله عنهما, who served the Prophet ﷺ pleasingly, reports the Prophet ﷺ twice commanding Abu Bakr to free Sad despite having no other servant, promising arrival of other men. > **""O Abu Bakr, free Sad. Free Sad, men shall come to you, free Sad, men shall come to you.""**"
"7249","Salamah ibn al-Akwa رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ owned a boy named Yasar, and upon seeing his excellent prayer, freed him."
"7250","Al-Hasan ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reports entering the ablution place and finding a morsel in the filth channel, removing harm, washing it well, and entrusting it to his boy for remembrance, but the boy ate it, whereupon Hasan freed him for Allah upon hearing from Fatimah bint Rasulillah رضي الله عنها from her father ﷺ that whoever lifts and purifies such a fallen morsel then eats it is forgiven before it settles, declaring he would not employ a man of Paradise. > **""Whoever takes a morsel or fragment from the channel of filth and urine, removes harm from it, washes it well, then eats it, it does not settle in his belly before he is forgiven.""**"
"7251","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports owing the freeing of a slave from the children of Ismail عليه السلام, intending to free among Yemeni Khawlan captives but being forbidden by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then being commanded to free among Mudar captives of Banu al-Anbar."
"7252","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that divorce is only for the waiting period and freeing only for the sake of Allah. > **""There is no divorce except for a waiting period, and no freeing except for the sake of Allah.""**"
"7253","Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring the freeing of a believing neck a ransom from the Fire. > **""Whoever frees a believing neck, it is his ransom from the Fire.""**"
"7254","Shubah al-Kufi reports at Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa's gathering his father's narration from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that freeing a neck frees each of the freer's limbs from the Fire for each of its limbs. > **""Whoever frees a neck, Allah frees for each of its limbs a limb of his from the Fire.""**"
"7255","Malik ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ promise binding Paradise to one who joins an orphan of Muslim parents to his food and drink until independent, and ransom from the Fire limb for limb to one who frees a Muslim person. > **""Whoever joins an orphan of Muslim parents to his food and drink until he is independent of him, Paradise becomes binding for him, and whoever frees a Muslim person, he is his ransom from the Fire, each of his limbs sufficing for a limb of his.""**"
"7256","Malik ibn Amr al-Qushayri رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare freeing a Muslim neck a ransom from the Fire with each bone for a bone. > **""Whoever frees a Muslim neck, it is his ransom from the Fire, and each bone of his bones is freed for a bone of his bones.""**"
"7257","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that freeing a believing neck flows through each limb in saving a limb from the Fire. > **""Whoever frees a believing neck, from each of its limbs a limb of his is freed from the Fire.""**"
"7258","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that freeing a neck for Allah frees each of the freer's limbs from the Fire for each of its limbs. > **""Whoever frees a neck for Allah, Allah frees for each of its limbs a limb of his from the Fire.""**"
"7259","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports being asked which night is most heard, receiving the last depth of night with accepted prayer until dawn, no prayer until the sun is a spear or two high, accepted prayer until the shadow stands a spear's length, none until the sun declines, accepted until a spear or two remains, then none until sunset, followed by ransom rulings for a Muslim man freeing a Muslim man, a Muslim woman freeing a Muslim woman, and one freeing two Muslim necks. > **""The last depth of the night. Then prayer is accepted until dawn breaks. Then no prayer until the sun is a spear or two high. Then prayer is accepted until the shadow stands a spear's length. Then no prayer until the sun declines. Then prayer is accepted until the sun is a spear or two high. Then no prayer until the sun sets.""** > **""Any Muslim man who frees a Muslim man, he is his ransom from the Fire, each bone of his sufficing for a bone of his, and any Muslim woman who frees a Muslim woman, she is her ransom from the Fire, each bone of hers sufficing for a bone of hers, and any Muslim man who frees two Muslim necks, they are his ransom from the Fire, each two bones of theirs sufficing for a bone of his.""**"
"7260","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that freeing a believer in this world frees each of the freer's limbs from the Fire for each of its limbs. > **""Whoever frees a believer in this world, Allah frees for each of its limbs a limb of his from the Fire.""**"
"7261","Abu Sukaynah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding one who owns enough for a neck's price to free it as limb-for-limb ransom. > **""When one of you owns enough for the price of a neck, let him free it, for each of its limbs ransoms a limb of his.""**"
"7262","A man of the Ansar رضي الله عنه reports bringing a black slave girl while owing a believing neck, asking the Prophet ﷺ to free her if believing, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ questioned her on the testimony of monotheism, his messengership, and resurrection, received her affirmation, and ordered her freeing. > **""Do you testify there is no god but Allah? Do you testify I am the Messenger of Allah? Do you believe in resurrection after death? Free her.""**"
"7263","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports a man owing a neck and possessing a black non-Arab slave girl, whom the Prophet ﷺ questioned on the two testimonies and ordered freed upon affirmation, with another version questioning where Allah is and receiving a gesture to the heaven and affirmation of his messengership. > **""Bring her to me. Do you testify there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? Free her. Where is Allah? Who am I? You are the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"7264","Abu Juhayfah رضي الله عنه reports a woman bringing a black slave girl while owing a believing neck and asking sufficiency, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ questioned her on Allah's aboveness, his identity, the two testimonies, and belief in revelation, received affirmation, and judged her believing for freeing. > **""Where is Allah? In the heaven. Who am I? You are the Messenger of Allah. Do you testify there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? Do you believe in what came from Allah? Free her, for she is a believer.""**"
"7265","Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah reports from his father from his grandfather that a woman brought a slave girl while owing a believing neck, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ questioned on her Lord, religion, his identity, testimony, five prayers, Ramadan fasting, and affirmation of revelation, then struck her back ordering her freeing as sufficient. > **""Who is your Lord? Allah is my Lord. What is your religion? Islam. Who am I? You are the Messenger of Allah. Do you testify I am the Messenger of Allah? I bear witness you are the Messenger of Allah. Do you pray the five? Do you fast Ramadan? Do you affirm what came from Allah? Free her, for she has sufficed for you.""**"
"7266","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to free slaves who came to him before their masters when they embraced Islam, freeing two men on the day of Taif."
"7267","A variant reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announcing on the day of Taif that any slave emerging is free, whereupon slaves including Abu Bakrah emerged and were freed. > **""Whoever among the slaves emerges is free.""**"
"7268","Al-Shabi reports from a Thaqafi man that they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ three concessions without license, concerning purification in cold land, gourds, and return of Abu Bakrah, who had emerged to the Prophet ﷺ during the Taif siege and embraced Islam, receiving the answer of Divine and Messengerial emancipation. > **""He is the freedman of Allah and the freedman of His Messenger.""**"
"7269","A variant via al-Shabi from a Thaqafi man reports requesting return of Abu Bakrah as a slave embracing before them, receiving refusal and the judgment of Divine then Messengerial freeing. > **""No, he is the freedman of Allah, then the freedman of the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"7270","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports emerging to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he besieged Taif with twenty-three slaves, whom the Messenger ﷺ freed, known thereafter as the Utaqa."
"7271","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighting at Taif and commanding a crier to announce that any slave emerging is free, whereupon two slaves emerged and were freed. > **""Any slave who emerges is free.""**"
"7272","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports a slave lowering himself from the Taif fortress, whose master demanded his return, receiving the ruling that a slave embracing before his master is not returned, but a slave embracing after his master is handed over. > **""When a slave embraces before his master, he is not returned to him, and when the master embraces then the slave embraces, he is handed over to him.""**"
"7273","Ghaylan ibn Salamah al-Thaqafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Nafi' had been his slave and fled to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, while Ghaylan was still a polytheist. When Ghaylan later accepted Islam, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, returned Nafi's wala' to him."
"7274","Abu al-Darda', may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ruled that one who trifles with divorce or emancipation is held to what he uttered. > **""Whoever trifles with divorce or emancipation, it is as he said.""**"
"7275","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that divorce is valid only after a marriage contract and emancipation only after ownership. > **""There is no divorce except after a contract, and no emancipation except after ownership.""**"
"7276","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded them to show favor to children born of zina by freeing them."
"7277","Salma bint Nasr al-Muharibiyyah said she asked 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning freeing a child born of zina, and she instructed her to free him."
"7278","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, said he contracted his mukatabah with his masters upon planting five hundred palm-shoots for them, such that when they took root he would be free. He mentioned this to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who instructed him to plant and stipulate for them and to inform him before stipulating. Salman informed him, the Prophet came and planted with his own hand all except one, which Salman planted himself, and all took root except that one. > **""Plant and stipulate for them, and when you intend to stipulate, inform me.""**"
"7279","Barirah, may Allah be pleased with her, said three Sunan were established through her: meat given to her in charity which she gifted to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who kept it until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, entered and asked about it; her mukatabah for nine uqiyyahs, when 'A'ishah offered to pay in one sum while her masters stipulated wala' for themselves, whereupon the Prophet commanded the condition be left and ruled wala' for the emancipator; and her being given the choice of marriage after her emancipation. > **""It is charity for Barirah and a gift for us. Leave the condition, leave it, for wala' belongs to the one who emancipates.""**"
"7280","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, bought Barirah from the Ansar to free her, and they stipulated her wala' for themselves, which she accepted. When the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, came, she informed him, and he ruled wala' for the emancipator, then ascended the minbar and censured those who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah, rejecting every such condition. Barirah had a husband, and the Messenger gave her the choice to remain with him or part from him, and she parted from him. Later, seeing a sheep's trotter in the house, and learning it was charity given to Barirah and gifted onward, he ordered it cooked as charity for her and gift for them. > **""Wala' belongs only to the one who emancipates. What is the matter with people who stipulate conditions not in the Book of Allah? Whatever condition is not in the Book of Allah is rejected back to the Book of Allah. Cook it, for it is charity for her and a gift for us.""**"
"7281","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said Barirah's husband was a black slave named Mughith, whom he saw weeping in the streets of Madinah. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave four judgments concerning her: despite her masters stipulating wala', he ruled it for the emancipator; he gave her the choice and she chose herself; he commanded her to observe 'iddah; and when charity given to her was gifted to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, he ruled it charity for Barirah and gift for them. > **""Wala' belongs to the one who emancipates. It is charity for her and a gift for us.""**"
"7282","Al-Suddi reports from his father that Zaynab bint Qays ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with her, contracted his mukatabah for ten thousand, and when payment fell due she remitted a thousand to him. She was among those who prayed toward both qiblahs with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him."
"7283","Isma'il ibn Umayyah reports from his grandfather that a slave called Tahman or Dhakwan had his share freed by his grandfather. The slave came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who ruled him partly free and partly enslaved, and he continued serving his master until he died. > **""You are free in respect of your person and enslaved in respect of your person.""**"
"7284","'Abdullah ibn Sinan al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, permitted a man to free of his slave whatever portion he willed, with no constraint between him and Allah. > **""A man may free of his slave whatever he wills — if he wills a third, if he wills a quarter, if he wills a fifth; there is no compulsion between him and Allah.""**"
"7285","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that a slave was jointly owned by ten men. Nine freed their shares while the tenth refused, saying, O Messenger of Allah, it is my eminence. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, affirmed his upper right in him remained. > **""Your upper share is in him.""**"
"7286","Muhammad ibn 'Umar ibn Sa'id reports that a slave jointly owned by ten was freed by all except one. He came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, seeking his intercession with that man. The Prophet spoke to him, the man gifted his share to the Prophet, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, freed him. He thereafter called himself the mawla of the Messenger of Allah. His name was Rafi', Abu al-Bahiyy."
"7287","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that a man of Hudhayl freed his share in a slave, whereupon the Prophet ruled him free, for Allah has no partner. > **""He is free, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has no partner.""**"
"7288","Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab said they preserved from thirty Companions of the Prophet, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ruled that whoever frees his share in a slave guarantees the remainder to the partners. > **""Whoever frees a share in a slave guarantees the remainder to them.""**"
"7289","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ruled that one who frees his portion in a slave guarantees the partners their portions from his own wealth. > **""Whoever frees a portion in a slave guarantees to them their portions from his wealth.""**"
"7290","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, imposed upon one who frees his share in a slave to free the remainder, and if he lacked means, the slave would work to earn his price. > **""Whoever frees a share he owns in a slave, upon him is to free the remainder; if he has no wealth, the slave shall work to earn his price.""**"
"7291","'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ruled that when a slave is jointly owned and one partner frees him, he is valued at the highest price, the emancipator pays his value, and the slave is freed. > **""When a slave is jointly owned and one of them frees him, he is valued at the highest value, so he pays his price and the slave is freed.""**"
"7292","'Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ruled that one who frees a share of a slave guarantees the remainder. > **""Whoever frees a share of a slave, he guarantees the remainder.""**"
"7293","In another wording from 'Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, imposed completion of the emancipation upon one who possesses means. > **""Upon him is the completion of his emancipation, if he has wealth.""**"
"7294","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said two men of Juhaynah jointly owned a slave, and one freed him. He came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who held him liable for his partner's share. He owned a small flock near a hundred sheep, which he sold and paid to his partner."
"7295","Khawwat ibn Jubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, said a man died entrusting him with his estate, including his umm walad and a free wife. Harsh words passed between the two women, and the free wife taunted her that tomorrow she would be seized by the ear and sold in the market. He mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who forbade her sale. > **""She is not to be sold.""**"
"7296","'Amrah reports that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, fell ill and her complaint lengthened. A practitioner came to Madinah, and her nephews asked him about her pain. He swore they described a bewitched woman. They identified her slave-girl as the sorceress. The girl confessed she had sought 'A'ishah's death in order to be freed, though she was already a mudabbara to be freed upon her mistress's death. 'A'ishah ordered her sold to the harshest Arab master and her price used for another like her."
